$DIR/main.p:15:9: cannot store &s in memory that lives longer, which outlives s
    &s points into s. s is a local variable of the function literal (declared at
    main.p:14). When the function literal returns, s's memory is reclaimed, but
    memory that lives longer may be on the heap and live longer, so it would be
    left pointing at reclaimed memory.
    Go would move this memory to the heap, and its garbage collector would free
    it later. Plutonium has no garbage collector and never moves memory to the
    heap behind the program's back, so that every allocation is visible in the
    code.
    Store values instead of views of s, or allocate s with new(S) and store its
    owner, an owned[S], in memory that lives longer.
    (see "Views" in the Plutonium spec, section 18.3)
    (run "pluto explain E3002" for a longer explanation)
